  "query_preview": "..High pressure aloft will dominate the weather across\nnorthern Arizona through early next week with a fair weather \nforecast through the Thanksgiving holiday weekend.\n\nHigh pressure aloft will become centered off the Baja California \ncoast on Thursday placing Arizona under northerly flow aloft. As\ntime goes on, a series of successively stronger shortwave troughs\nwill push the high southward. Initially, the origins of the flow \nwill be from over the Great Basin with a history of \nsubsidence/warming. As a result, expect warming daytime \ntemperatures, eventually climb to 5-15 degrees above average \nFriday and over the weekend. Cooler temperatures will then arrive\nearly next week with the stronger shortwave troughs. On Thursday \nand Friday surface pressure gradients will favor north to \nnortheast winds at 5-15 mph. Under this pattern local nocturnal \nnortheast winds at 10-20 mph are forecast along and south of the \nMogollon Rim. From Saturday onward, as the high center shifts \nsouthward, winds will develop a westerly component generally light\nat 5-15 mph on Saturday and Sunday but becoming west to southwest\nat 10-20 mph gusting to 30 mph by Monday or Tuesday. There will \nbe periods of mid- to high- level cloudiness with each passing \nshortwave, most extensive Wednesday night, Saturday into Sunday \nand from later on Monday into Tuesday. \n\nFrom Wednesday onward...Models show the passing troughs becoming\neven stronger delivering gusty winds, cooler temperatures and\nincreasing chances for precipitation.",
